'''James Fiennes, 1st Baron Saye and Sele''' (22 September 1395 – 4 July 1450) was an English soldier and politician. He was born at [[Herstmonceux]], Sussex, the second son of Sir William Fiennes (1 August 1357 – 18 January 1402) and his wife Elizabeth Batisford (Wartling, Sussex, 1363 - Herstmonceux, 18 January 1405).

Fiennes fought in the [[Hundred Years' War]] and served as [[High Sheriff of Kent]] in 1436 and [[High Sheriff of Surrey]] and [[High Sheriff of Sussex|Sussex]] in 1438.<ref>{{cite web | url= http://www.highsheriffs.com/Kent/KentHistory.htm | title= Kent County History | publisher= The High Sheriffs Association of England and Wales | access-date= 10 April 2011 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110927155026/http://www.highsheriffs.com/Kent/KentHistory.htm | archive-date= 27 September 2011 | url-status= dead }}</ref> He was [[Constable of Dover Castle]] and [[Lord Warden of the Cinque Ports|Warden of the Cinque Ports]] from 1447 to 1450, and [[Lord High Treasurer of England]] from 1449 to 1450. Fiennes' tenure as Lord High Treasurer occurred during the [[Great Bullion Famine]] and the [[Great Slump (15th century)|Great Slump in England]].

He was summoned to Parliament from 1446 to 1449 and is said to have been created [[Baron Saye and Sele]] by [[letters patent]] in 1447. Saye and Sele was a supporter of [[William de la Pole, 1st Duke of Suffolk]], the principal power behind the throne of [[Henry VI of England|Henry VI]].

After Suffolk's deposition and murder in 1450, Fiennes was imprisoned in the [[Tower of London|Tower]] with his son-in-law [[William Cromer (died 1450)|William Cromer]], deputy-sheriff of Kent in 1444.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.warsoftheroses.co.uk/chapter_39.htm|publisher= Michael D. Miller|title= Wars of the Roses|access-date=31 October 2017}}</ref> Having been released from the tower and handed over to the rebels as a placatory gesture by the King, Baron Saye was brought to [[Guildhall, London|Guildhall]] for a sham trial. Upon being found guilty of treason, he was paraded through part of London and beheaded by a mob of the rebels in London under [[Jack Cade]] at the Standard in [[Cheapside]] on 4 July 1450.<ref>Harvey, I. M. W. (1991) ''Jack Cade's Rebellion of 1450.'' Oxford: Clarendon Press, p. 93.</ref> 

His son-in-law was also executed by the rebels outside the city walls on the same day. The heads of the two men were put on pikes and unceremoniously paraded through the streets of London while their bearers pushed them together so that they appeared to kiss.<ref>Griffiths, Ralph A. (1981). ''The Reign of King Henry VI: The Exercise of Royal Authority, 1422–1461.'' Berkeley: University of California Press, p. 618.</ref> Fiennes was subsequently buried in the [[Christ_Church_Greyfriars|Franciscan church at Newgate]]. Shortly after the burial, English settlers dispossessed from their property in Bayeux and Caen (who had recently arrived in London) removed his coat of arms from the pillar by his grave and reversed it - an act intended to dishonour the dead. <ref>{{cite book |last1=Sumption |first1=Jonathan |title=Triumph and Illusion (The Hundred Years War V) |date=2023 |publisher=Faber & Faber Ltd |location=London |isbn=978-0-571-27457-4 |page=711}}</ref>.He was succeeded in the barony by his son [[William Fiennes, 2nd Baron Saye and Sele|William]].

